{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-85ad4d8e0d93767c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018 (GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators)\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done. Strategy priority: Major.\n13\t\n14\tThe strategy builds administrator-facing GPU chargeback reporting on top of the\n15\tper-project GPU-hour metering data delivered by the in-flight strategy\n16\tRHAISTRAT-9990. Metering collection is explicitly out of scope here; this\n17\tstrategy consumes its data.\n18\t\n19\t## Epic List\n20\t\n21\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n22\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Per-project GPU-hour consumption reporting backend (reports, stable API, CSV export) | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Dashboard admin view for GPU chargeback reporting (time-range reports + quota comparison) | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | GPU chargeback reporting documentation (report semantics + export integration) | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | P1 |\n26\t\n27\t## Dependency DAG\n28\t\n29\t```mermaid\n30\tgraph TD\n31\t    E001[\"RHAISTRAT-9018-E001<br/>Consumption reporting backend\"] --> E002[\"RHAISTRAT-9018-E002<br/>Dashboard admin view\"]\n32\t    E001 --> E003[\"RHAISTRAT-9018-E003<br/>Documentation\"]\n33\t    E002 --> E003\n34\t```\n35\t\n36\t## DAG Justification\n37\t\n38\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n39\t|------|------|-----------|\n40\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (implementation producing an artifact another epic's code builds against) | The Dashboard admin view builds against the stable reporting API and report data model produced by the reporting backend; the consumer cannot be built until the API artifact exists. |\n41\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation must describe the delivered report semantics and export surface. |\n42\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation must describe the delivered admin view, including the quota-comparison display. |\n43\t\n44\tNo other edges: the two implementation epics belong to different components and\n45\tteams (Rule 1), and the only shared artifact between them is the reporting API,\n46\talready covered by the single E001 → E002 edge. No `konflux-onboarding`,\n47\t`repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics exist — both components are\n48\testablished, GA, and Konflux-onboarded per architecture context, so Rules 5–10\n49\tdo not apply.\n50\t\n51\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n52\t\n53\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic(s) | Notes |\n54\t|-----|----------|-------------|---------|-------|\n55\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Administrators can view per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges, computed from the metering data delivered by RHAISTRAT-9990. | RHAISTRAT-9018-E001, RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Split along the architectural boundary: E001 delivers the report computation and stable retrieval API from the metering data; E002 delivers the administrator-facing viewing surface in the Dashboard. Both epics are P0 — the HLR is not satisfied without either half. |\n56\t| HLR-2 | P1 | Reports can be exported as CSV and retrieved via a stable API for finance tooling. | RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: CSV export and the stable API are presentation/transport modes of the same report data structure and computation delivered for HLR-1; a split would build the same report surface twice in the same epic's code paths. |\n57\t| HLR-3 | P1 | The Dashboard admin view shows current-month consumption against each project's Kueue quota. | RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: the quota comparison renders in the same Dashboard admin view surface built for HLR-1's time-range reports; a split would build the same UI surface twice. |\n58\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation covers report semantics (what counts as a GPU-hour) and export integration. | RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Separate docs-authoring epic — substantive new content authoring is a distinct work-product type (Rule: work-product type boundaries). Epic priority is P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Major → P1), not inherited from the implementations. |\n59\t\n60\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n61\t\n62\t## Component Mapping Notes\n63\t\n64\tCanonical components (from `.context/rhai-components.txt`): the strategy's\n65\tAffected Components list (`Dashboard`, `Distributed Workloads`) maps verbatim to\n66\tcanonical names — no paraphrasing needed. Non-obvious assignments:\n67\t\n68\t- The strategy lists both components but contains no technical approach, so it\n69\t  does not state where the report computation backend lives. The reporting\n70\t  backend (computation, API, CSV export) is assigned to **Distributed\n71\t  Workloads** — the component and team that own the GPU workload/quota domain\n72\t  (Kueue ClusterQueue/LocalQueue, per\n73\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/distributed-workloads.md`)\n74\t  and the metering adjacency (RHAISTRAT-9990). The Dashboard owns only the\n75\t  admin UI (it owns no data plane, per\n76\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`).\n77\t- The documentation epic uses the canonical **Documentation** component; the\n78\t  strategy does not name a docs team, so a default docs team is assigned (see\n79\t  Health Warnings).\n80\t\n81\t## Health Warnings\n82\t\n83\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n84\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, no Non-Functional\n85\t  Requirements, and no technical approach section. Decomposition proceeded\n86\t  from the HLR table, affected-components list, and open questions. Team\n87\t  assignments were inferred from the architecture-context owning teams (RHOAI\n88\t  Dashboard for Dashboard; Distributed Workloads for Distributed Workloads);\n89\t  the docs epic team is a default (RHOAI Docs) and should be confirmed.\n90\t- **External dependency timing (RHAISTRAT-9990)**: the metering data schema\n91\t  finalizes only when the in-flight GPU usage metering strategy lands. This is\n92\t  the strategy's single flagged open question, and its resolution is external —\n93\t  it is owned by RHAISTRAT-9990, not resolvable by investigation work inside\n94\t  this strategy, so it is modeled as a precondition on the reporting backend\n95\t  epic (RHAISTRAT-9018-E001) rather than an Investigation epic or a DAG edge:\n96\t  that epic carries an acceptance criterion that reports are computed from the\n97\t  finalized metering schema (with drift absorbed in the consumption layer) and\n98\t  a risk note on the external timing. The answer does not change which epics\n99\t  exist or their boundaries — the reporting backend, Dashboard view, and docs\n100\t  epics exist either way — so per the Step 3 decision rule it is an acceptance\n101\t  criterion, not an Investigation.\n102\t- **No priority inversions detected**: P0 work (report computation + admin\n103\t  viewing) is correctly the critical path; P1 export/API and quota comparison\n104\t  are bundled into the P0 epics on shared surfaces (see HLR Traceability\n105\t  Matrix); P2 documentation is a separate epic at P1 per the docs-authoring\n106\t  priority exception.\n107\t- **Epic size**: no effort estimates are given in the strategy; both\n108\t  implementation epics are scoped to a single component and a single logical\n109\t  change each and are assumed within the ~2-week guideline. No re-split\n110\t  triggers recorded.\n111\t\n112\t## Tiered Delivery\n1