{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a595067b0ef7b69b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9018\n10\t\n11\t## Review Summary\n12\t\n13\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n14\tRecommendation: accept\n15\t\n16\tcontract validator: OK (skills/epic-decompose/scripts/validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-9018 exited 0 — body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type parallelism, re-split triggers, and bundled-flag recording all pass).\n17\t\n18\tTriage check: `triage: null` (full decomposition); 3 epics is expected for a non-triaged strategy, no contradiction.\n19\t\n20\t## Criterion Details\n21\t\n22\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n23\tAll four HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 (P0) split along the architectural boundary across E001 (computation/retrieval) and E002 (viewing surface); HLR-2 (P1) bundled into E001 with an explicit `bundled` flag and rationale in the matrix row; HLR-3 (P1) bundled into E002 likewise; HLR-4 (P2) in E003 with the docs-authoring priority exception correctly applied (strategy priority Major → P1). Priority inheritance holds: E001 (P0) blocks the P0 E002; E003 is a docs-authoring epic exempt from inheritance. The P1 bundling is not priority collapse: HLR-2's stable API is load-bearing for the P0 viewing surface (E002 retrieves through it) and CSV export is a transport mode of the same report data structure; HLR-3's quota comparison renders in the same Dashboard admin view surface built for HLR-1 — both are presentation modes of one surface, not independently deferrable features, so merging is the correct shape (a split would duplicate surface construction).\n24\t\n25\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n26\tNo circular dependencies. All three edges are justified: E001 → E002 under Rule 13 (E002's code builds against the stable reporting API artifact E001 produces — a genuine code artifact dependency, not a build-pipeline output consumed as content); E001 → E003 and E002 → E003 under Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations). Frontmatter `dependencies` match the summary diagram exactly in both directions, with arrows drawn dependency → dependent per convention. No konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist (both components are established and onboarded per architecture context), so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism is not applicable and no edge serializes mandated-parallel work. Critical path length 3 (E001 → E002 → E003) is appropriate for a strategy of this size with docs-last ordering. No branch files exist, so branch/gated_by checks are N/A. Cross-check of epic content found no uncaptured data dependencies: E002's Kueue quota model is an existing platform surface, not an epic-produced artifact.\n27\t\n28\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n29\tEach epic has a distinct component/team tuple: E001 (Distributed Workloads / Distributed Workloads), E002 (Dashboard / RHOAI Dashboard), E003 (Documentation / RHOAI Docs) — all component names canonical in `.context/rhai-components.txt`, and team assignments match the architecture-context owning teams. No scope duplication: E001 computes and serves reports, E002 explicitly renders via the reporting API with \"no parallel consumption computation,\" E003 authors content only. No work-product type mixing — the docs-authoring epic is separate from the code epics. Both implementation epics are scoped to one component and one logical change; no size overages, so no re-split triggers are needed (and none are recorded, correctly).\n30\t\n31\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n32\tNo Investigation epics, and none are warranted: the strategy's single open question (metering schema finalization timing) is owned by the external RHAISTRAT-9990 strategy — no investigation inside this strategy can resolve it, and its resolution does not change which epics exist or their boundaries, so modeling it as an acceptance criterion plus risk note on E001 is the correct Step 3 outcome. Both Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (reporting backend with API/CSV; Dashboard admin view). `gated_by` is null everywhere with no Investigation dependencies, so no gating metadata is required.\n33\t\n34\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n35\tAll three epics are typed Implementation and carry `ai_signals` (the type-appropriate field); none carry `investigation_signals`. Frontmatter values match the ai-signals rationale files exactly. E001's `open_questions: -1` and `external_dependency: -1` are consistent with the strategy's flagged open question on the unfinalized metering schema; E002's `open_questions: 0` / `external_dependency: 0` are defensible since the API contract arrives as an in-strategy dependency. Architecture-claims calibration verified against `.context`: E001 and E002 score 1 with citations to `distributed-workloads.md` and `dashboard.md`, whose contents corroborate the cited premises (GA maturity, ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota model, Kueue metrics in Prometheus, Dashboard owning no data plane, OdhDashboardConfig feature flags). E003's `architecture_claims: 0` is not a calibration miss — no architecture-context file exists for the Documentation component and its facts derive from the implemented epics at review time. Per instructions, computed scores/thresholds were not re-derived.\n36\t\n37\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n38\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs are present: E003 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-implementation AC; E002 carries a feature-flag (OdhDashboardConfig, disabled by default) AC; E001 and E002 each keep the existing build/test pipeline green. E001's schema AC is a condition on the epic's own deliverable (the consumption layer absorbing drift without changing the API contract), and the